---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import synMriPipeline from '../../assets/research/synthetic_mri_pipeline.png';
import synMriResults from '../../assets/research/synthetic_mri_results.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Synthetic MRI"
  description="A pipeline that synthesises physics-consistent MR images and raw multi-coil k-space from quantitative multi-parameter maps, for building training and benchmarking datasets."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2025 · Max Planck Institute for Human Cognitive and Brain Sciences</p>
      <h1>Synthetic Image and Multi-Coil K-space Generation From Multi-Parameter Maps</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        Deep-learning reconstruction needs large, diverse raw MRI datasets that are slow and
        expensive to acquire. This pipeline generates them from an analytic signal model —
        physics-consistent images <em>and</em> the multi-coil k-space behind them, with ground
        truth tailored to the sequence, coil sensitivities, and noise.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Research intern · with K. Pine and N. Weiskopf</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">quantitative MRI</span>
            <span class="tag">k-space simulation</span>
            <span class="tag">coil sensitivities</span>
            <span class="tag">signal modelling</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Materials</dt>
          <dd class="links">
            <a href="https://echo.ismrm.org/conferences/ISMRM2026/digital-poster/13628" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISMRM 2026 poster ↗</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/borankilic/synthetic_mri" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code (GitHub) ↗</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The idea</h2>
      <p>
        Quantitative multi-parameter mapping (MPM) measures tissue properties — longitudinal
        and effective transverse relaxation rates <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>,
        <em>R</em><sub>2</sub><sup>*</sup>, and proton density — that are independent of the
        sequence used to read them. Given those maps, the signal any sequence <em>would</em>
        produce can be computed forward. For a spoiled gradient echo, for instance, the
        signal follows the familiar FLASH expression
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>S</em> ∝ PD · sin<em>α</em> ·
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">1 − <em>e</em><sup>−<em>TR·R</em><sub>1</sub></sup></span><span class="den">1 − cos<em>α</em> · <em>e</em><sup>−<em>TR·R</em><sub>1</sub></sup></span></span>
        · <em>e</em><sup>−<em>TE·R</em><sub>2</sub><sup>*</sup></sup>
      </div>

      <p>
        so sweeping TR, TE, and flip angle generates arbitrary contrasts from one acquisition.
        The harder — and more useful — part is going all the way to <em>raw</em> data:
        reintroducing the transmit-field inhomogeneity <em>B</em><sub>1</sub><sup>+</sup>,
        applying per-coil sensitivity maps, accruing the TE-dependent phase from the off-resonance
        field Δ<em>B</em><sub>0</sub>, and adding correlated noise before the Fourier transform.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={synMriPipeline} alt="Pipeline schematic: multi-parameter maps plus B1, B0, sensitivity, and noise data feeding synthetic image and multi-coil k-space generation." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          The pipeline. Multi-parameter maps plus measured B₁⁺, ΔB₀, coil-sensitivity and noise
          data produce per-coil complex images and matching k-space, with adjustable
          TR / TE / FA / TI and sampling pattern.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="results" />

      <h2>What comes out</h2>
      <p>
        Flip angles are modulated voxelwise by the coregistered
        <em>B</em><sub>1</sub><sup>+</sup> map so the nominal angle reflects the real one;
        sensitivity maps are estimated with ESPIRiT and lightly smoothed to reduce calibration
        noise while preserving coil structure; and multi-echo phase is unwrapped with ROMEO
        before linear estimation of Δ<em>B</em><sub>0</sub>. Synthesised FLASH, FSE, FLAIR, and
        MP2RAGE contrasts reproduce expected tissue contrast across parameter sweeps, and the
        resulting per-coil k-space matches the spatial-frequency content and coil modulation of
        measured acquisitions.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={synMriResults} alt="Grid of synthetic MR contrasts — FLASH, FLAIR, FSE, MP2RAGE — with example per-coil images and their k-spaces." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Original MPMs (left) are the inputs; the remaining columns show synthesised FLASH,
          FLAIR, FSE and MP2RAGE contrasts, example per-coil images, and their corresponding
          k-spaces — all generated from the same maps.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Synthesised k-spaces survive a round trip: reconstructed and fed back through a
        quantitative fitting pipeline, they recover multi-parameter maps approximating the
        originals, demonstrating bidirectional consistency. Undersampled data reconstructed with
        LORAKS remain visually and structurally close to fully sampled reference images, which is
        what makes the framework usable for benchmarking reconstruction algorithms.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limits</h2>
      <p>
        The realism is bounded by the input maps and calibration quality. Imperfect
        <em>B</em><sub>1</sub><sup>+</sup> estimation produces flip-angle bias; errors in MPM
        fitting or coregistration propagate through; the noise model can underestimate inter-coil
        correlations; and the linear Δ<em>B</em><sub>0</sub> phase approximation fails in strongly
        off-resonant or multi-echo regimes. Physical effects outside the model — intravoxel
        dephasing, eddy currents, flow, motion — are simply absent. The framework is a practical
        baseline to prototype on and extend, not a substitute for a full Bloch simulator.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .shell { max-width: 44rem; }
  .back { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .back a, .proj-foot a { color: var(--muted); }
  .back a:hover, .proj-foot a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  .proj-head { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .proj-head h1 { margin: 0.15em 0 0.35em; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.12; }
  .proj-blurb { font-size: 1.18rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 40rem; }
  .proj-meta {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0; display: grid; gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .proj-meta > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .proj-meta dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
  }
  .proj-meta dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 0.5rem; }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--accent-deep); background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 3px;
  }
  .links { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; align-items: start; }
  .links a { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.86rem; }

  .fig { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block; width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--faint); border-radius: 4px; background: #fff;
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.6rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .eq {
    margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    text-align: center; font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.9;
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .eq em { font-style: italic; }
  .frac { display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; margin: 0 0.15rem; }
  .frac .num { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 0 0.35rem 0.1rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .frac .den { padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem 0; line-height: 1.35; }

  .proj-foot {
    margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .proj-meta > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
